---
import ModuleLayout from '../../layouts/ModuleLayout.astro';
import modules from '../../data/modules.json';
import '../../styles/modules/painting.css';
import '../../styles/icons/painting.css';

const mod = modules.find((m) => m.id === 32)!;
---

<ModuleLayout mod={mod}>


  <div class="painting-row">
    <i class="fa-solid fa-brush module-header-icon-accent"></i>
    <div>
      <div class="painting-text-2">Module 32 — Deep Dive</div>
      <h1 class="module-title module-heading">Painting</h1>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="module-subtitle">From pigment to meaning — the oldest and deepest visual art</p>
<!-- PART A -->
  <div id="partA">
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-a-what-painting-is-the-foundations">Part A · what painting is — the foundations</h2>
    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">The oldest surviving art form</p>
      <p class="cval">Pigment, surface, and human intention — 40,000 years of continuous practice</p>
      <p class="cnote">Painting is the application of pigment suspended in a binder to a surface with the intention of creating an image, pattern, or expressive mark. The oldest known paintings are in El Castillo, Spain (~40,800 BCE) and Lascaux, France (~17,000 BCE). The act of marking a surface deliberately is one of the most fundamental human behaviours: before writing, before agriculture, there was painting. Understanding painting means understanding how material constraints, cultural context, available technology, and individual genius interact to produce images that outlast civilisations. This module covers every major dimension: from the chemistry of linseed oil to the economics of Christie's, from Brunelleschi's vanishing point to Pollock's drip technique.</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="three-dimensions-that-define-every-painting">Three dimensions that define every painting</h2>
    <div class="painting-panel">
      <div class="dim-tabs">
        <button class="dim-tab active" onclick="showDim('material',this)">Material</button>
        <button class="dim-tab" onclick="showDim('formal',this)">Formal</button>
        <button class="dim-tab" onclick="showDim('icono',this)">Iconographic</button>
      </div>
      <div id="dim-material" class="dim-panel show">
        <div class="painting-grid">
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Pigment</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">The colouring substance — mineral, organic, or synthetic. Lapis lazuli ultramarine was more expensive than gold per ounce in 14th-century Florence. Lead white, vermilion (mercury sulphide), and malachite green dominated for centuries. Modern painters have thousands of lightfast synthetic pigments unavailable to the Old Masters.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Binder</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">The medium that holds pigment together and adheres it to the surface. Egg yolk (tempera), linseed or walnut oil, gum arabic (watercolour), acrylic polymer emulsion. The binder determines working time, surface texture, and how the painting ages — oil yellows; tempera does not; acrylics dry to a plastic film.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Support</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">The surface painted on. Cave wall, wooden panel (gessoed), linen or cotton canvas, paper, copper plate, plaster (fresco). Each support imposes constraints: canvas can roll up for transport; fresco bonds chemically to wet plaster within hours and cannot be moved without heroic conservation.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="dim-formal" class="dim-panel">
        <div class="painting-grid">
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Composition</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">How elements are arranged within the picture plane. Triangular compositions create stability; diagonals create tension; all-over compositions (Pollock) eliminate hierarchy entirely. These are structural choices with emotional consequences — not rules to follow but options to understand.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Colour relationships</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">Hue, saturation, value (lightness/darkness), temperature (warm/cool). The same grey looks different next to red than next to blue. Josef Albers spent his career demonstrating that colour is always relational — there is no fixed colour experience, only relationships between adjacent colours.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Mark and surface</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">Brushstroke, palette knife, finger, poured paint, spray. Visible marks record the gesture of making; smooth surfaces deny the process. The presence or absence of mark-evidence is a statement about whether the painting presents itself as made or as simply there.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div id="dim-icono" class="dim-panel">
        <div class="painting-grid">
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Genre hierarchy</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">The classical French Academy ranked genres: history painting (religious, mythological, historical) at the top; portrait; genre (everyday life); landscape; still life at the bottom. This hierarchy survived c.1660–1850. Impressionism inverted it by making everyday life and landscape the primary subjects.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Symbols and iconography</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">Objects that carry shared meanings within a tradition. Skull = memento mori. Lily = purity, the Virgin Mary. Hourglass = fleeting time. Dog = fidelity. Reading a painting iconographically means knowing its symbolic vocabulary — widespread among 17th-century viewers, largely lost today.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sub-card-roomy">
            <div class="detail-label-spaced">Historical context</div>
            <div class="painting-text-4">Who commissioned it and why. For what physical space. In what political moment. Goya's Third of May (1808) is incomprehensible without the Napoleonic invasion of Spain. Context does not determine meaning but is always present, shaping what could be said and how it could be received.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cscale-wrap">
      <div class="cscale-title">Major world painting traditions — representational vs expressive/symbolic orientation</div>
      <div class="painting-text-5">Characteristic tendencies of each tradition. Individual artists within every tradition have always pushed against its conventions.</div>
      <div class="scale-head"><span>Optical fidelity to visible world &larr;</span><span class="scale-head-line"></span><span>Symbolic / expressive</span></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Dutch Golden Age</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Renaissance Italy</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-2"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Impressionism</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-3"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Mughal miniature</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-4"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Chinese ink painting</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-5"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Byzantine icon</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-6"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Abstract Expressionism</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-7"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-poles"><span>&#x2190; Optical fidelity to the visible world</span><span>Internal emotion / spiritual meaning &#x2192;</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="sec" id="what-makes-painting-distinct-from-all-other-visual-arts">What makes painting distinct from all other visual arts</h2>
    <div class="painting-grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">Singular object</p>
        <p class="cval">Structural scarcity — the unrepeatable original</p>
        <p class="cnote">Unlike printmaking, photography, or film, a painting is typically a single unrepeatable object. The Mona Lisa is one panel of poplar wood in one room in the Louvre. This singularity drives the economics structurally. Walter Benjamin called this quality "aura": the presence of an original in time and space that reproduction cannot replicate. Every painting ages uniquely, accumulating its own history of handling, fading, and restoration.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">Time collapsed</p>
        <p class="cval">A frozen moment that accumulates duration</p>
        <p class="cnote">A painting does not unfold in time like music, literature, or film. You read it in any order. Yet it accumulates time — pentimento (painted-over earlier decisions) becomes visible after decades as upper layers become translucent. The cracks of centuries, the fading of pigments: the object embodies its own history in material form.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">The indexical mark</p>
        <p class="cval">The physical trace of a specific human hand</p>
        <p class="cnote">A painting retains the physical traces of its making: the directional drag of a brush, the pressure of a palette knife, the hesitation and revision. Rembrandt's impasto — paint so thick it casts shadows — is the direct record of his wrist gesture on a specific afternoon. This indexical quality distinguishes painting from photography, where light exposes the film without a human hand intervening.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">The fundamental illusion</p>
        <p class="cval">Two dimensions implying three — or refusing to</p>
        <p class="cnote">The core technical challenge of Western painting for five centuries: creating convincing three-dimensional space on a flat surface. Linear perspective (Brunelleschi, c.1420), aerial perspective (Leonardo), foreshortening, and chiaroscuro are all solutions. Modernism largely rejected the problem as a cultural premise rather than a perceptual necessity.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
  <!-- PART B -->
  <div id="partB">
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-b-materials-and-technique-how-medium-shapes-what-can-be-said">Part B · materials and technique — how medium shapes what can be said</h2>
    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Medium is not neutral</p>
      <p class="cval">Every technique imposes constraints that become aesthetic choices</p>
      <p class="cnote">The medium a painter uses is not a delivery vehicle for ideas that could equally be expressed another way. Oil paint, because it stays workable for days, allows blended transitions and endless revision — this produced the sfumato of Leonardo and the glazed luminosity of Vermeer. Fresco, because it dries in hours, demands decisive fast strokes and simplified forms — this shaped the monumental gravity of Michelangelo. When artists choose a medium in the modern era, with all options available, that choice is a statement about what kind of mark they want to make and how they want to be understood.</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="the-five-core-media-an-explorer">The five core media — an explorer</h2>
    <div class="explorer-wrap">
      <div class="exp-btns" id="medBtns">
        <button class="exp-btn active" onclick="showMedium('fresco',this)">Fresco</button>
        <button class="exp-btn" onclick="showMedium('tempera',this)">Egg Tempera</button>
        <button class="exp-btn" onclick="showMedium('oil',this)">Oil</button>
        <button class="exp-btn" onclick="showMedium('watercolour',this)">Watercolour</button>
        <button class="exp-btn" onclick="showMedium('acrylic',this)">Acrylic</button>
      </div>
      <div id="medPanel"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="slider-widget">
      <div class="painting-text-6">Practical drying time — from touch-dry to fully cured</div>
      <label for="drySlider">Drag across the timeline — each position reveals how working time shapes the painter's method:</label>
      <input class="u-w-100pct" type="range" id="drySlider" min="0" max="4" step="1" value="0">
      <div class="slider-poles"><span>Minutes (fresco plaster)</span><span>Months (oil fully polymerised)</span></div>
      <div class="slider-output" id="dryOutput"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="schematic-wrap">
      <div class="schematic-title">Anatomy of a prepared oil painting — layer cross-section</div>
      <svg class="painting-media" viewBox="0 0 580 200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect x="105" y="158" width="460" height="16" rx="2" fill="#8B6914" opacity=".4"/>
        <text x="100" y="170" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Canvas weave</text>
        <rect x="105" y="143" width="460" height="15" rx="1" fill="#C8A65A" opacity=".3"/>
        <text x="100" y="155" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Size (rabbit skin glue)</text>
        <rect x="105" y="123" width="460" height="20" rx="1" fill="#E8E0CC" opacity=".55"/>
        <text x="100" y="137" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Ground / gesso</text>
        <rect x="105" y="109" width="460" height="14" rx="1" fill="#D4A97A" opacity=".45"/>
        <text x="100" y="120" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Imprimatura (tinted wash)</text>
        <rect x="105" y="91" width="460" height="18" rx="1" fill="#6A8CA0" opacity=".45"/>
        <text x="100" y="104" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Underpaint (dead colour)</text>
        <rect x="105" y="62" width="460" height="29" rx="1" fill="#3A8C5E" opacity=".5"/>
        <text x="100" y="80" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Paint layers (opaque)</text>
        <rect x="105" y="47" width="460" height="15" rx="1" fill="#A04E30" opacity=".38"/>
        <text x="100" y="58" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Glazes (transparent)</text>
        <rect x="105" y="32" width="460" height="15" rx="1" fill="var(--accent)" opacity=".3"/>
        <text x="100" y="44" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="end" opacity=".65" font-family="sans-serif">Varnish (reversible)</text>
        <text x="335" y="18" font-size="10" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle" opacity=".45" font-family="sans-serif">viewer's eye / surface</text>
        <text x="335" y="192" font-size="10" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle" opacity=".4" font-family="sans-serif">structural support</text>
      </svg>
      <div class="painting-text-7">Each layer has a purpose. The size seals canvas fibres against oil absorption (oil left unsized rots canvas within decades). The ground creates a stable absorbent surface. The imprimatura — a tinted transparent wash — unifies the tonal base; Velazquez often used a warm reddish-brown that glows through upper layers. Glazes are transparent colour layers over dry paint: Vermeer achieved the luminosity of flesh in Girl with a Pearl Earring through multiple such layers. Varnish is replaced in conservation when it yellows.</div>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="what-paints-are-made-of-today-a-compositional-breakdown">What paints are made of today — a compositional breakdown</h2>
    <div class="painting-panel-3">
      <div class="painting-text-8">Approximate breakdown of pigment types in a professional-grade oil paint range (by count of distinct pigments):</div>
      <div class="dist-bar">
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style">Synthetic organic</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-2">Inorganic mineral</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-3">Iron oxide</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-4">Carbon black</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-5">Historic</div>
      </div>
      <div class="dist-legend u-mt-sm">
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style"></div>Synthetic organic — 38%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-2"></div>Inorganic mineral — 22%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-3"></div>Iron oxide family — 18%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-4"></div>Carbon blacks — 12%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-5"></div>Historic / traditional — 10%</div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-note">Synthetic organic pigments (phthalo blue, quinacridone red, dioxazine violet) were invented after 1900 and are more lightfast and intense than most historical equivalents. Lead white dominated for centuries — now classified hazardous and replaced by titanium white in most ranges, though many oil painters still prefer lead white for its handling. Genuine lapis lazuli ultramarine is still produced and now costs more per gram than gold.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- PART C -->
  <div id="partC">
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-c-how-to-read-a-painting-the-art-historian-s-toolkit">Part C · how to read a painting — the art historian's toolkit</h2>
    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Panofsky's three levels — still the best starting framework</p>
      <p class="cval">Pre-iconographic (what you see) / Iconographic (what it is) / Iconological (what it means culturally)</p>
      <p class="cnote">Erwin Panofsky's 1939 framework remains the most useful structure for approaching any painting. Level 1 (pre-iconographic): what do you literally see? Shapes, colours, gestures. Level 2 (iconographic): what are these things? A saint performing a miracle — identifiable from attributes and conventional poses. Level 3 (iconological): what is the deeper cultural meaning? The painting as a document of theological belief, patronage networks, and social anxiety. Most art writing operates at Level 2. Real interpretation requires Level 3 — understanding symbols within their historical and cultural context. The same skull means something different in a 17th-century Dutch vanitas still life, a Mexican Day of the Dead painting, and a Damien Hirst spot painting.</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="the-formal-elements-click-any-card-to-explore-in-depth">The formal elements — click any card to explore in depth</h2>
    <div class="elem-grid" id="formalGrid">
      <div class="elem-card" onclick="showFormalElem('line',this)">
        <div class="elem-card-icon elem-card-icon-style">&#8212;</div>
        <div class="elem-card-label">Line</div>
        <div class="elem-card-sub">direction and contour</div>
      </div>
      <div class="elem-card" onclick="showFormalElem('colour',this)">
        <div class="elem-card-icon elem-card-icon-style-2">&#x25CF;</div>
        <div class="elem-card-label">Colour</div>
        <div class="elem-card-sub">hue, value, saturation</div>
      </div>
      <div class="elem-card" onclick="showFormalElem('tone',this)">
        <div class="elem-card-icon elem-card-icon-style-3">&#x25D0;</div>
        <div class="elem-card-label">Tone</div>
        <div class="elem-card-sub">light and dark</div>
      </div>
      <div class="elem-card" onclick="showFormalElem('texture',this)">
        <div class="elem-card-icon elem-card-icon-style-4">&#x2248;</div>
        <div class="elem-card-label">Texture</div>
        <div class="elem-card-sub">surface and impasto</div>
      </div>
      <div class="elem-card" onclick="showFormalElem('space',this)">
        <div class="elem-card-icon elem-card-icon-style-5">&#x2B21;</div>
        <div class="elem-card-label">Space</div>
        <div class="elem-card-sub">depth and flatness</div>
      </div>
      <div class="elem-card" onclick="showFormalElem('composition',this)">
        <div class="elem-card-icon elem-card-icon-style-6">&#x229E;</div>
        <div class="elem-card-label">Composition</div>
        <div class="elem-card-sub">arrangement and rhythm</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="elem-detail" id="formalDetail">
      <div class="elem-detail-title" id="formalTitle"></div>
      <div class="elem-detail-body" id="formalBody"></div>
      <div class="elem-detail-example" id="formalExample"></div>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="chiaroscuro-light-and-dark-as-expressive-language">Chiaroscuro — light and dark as expressive language</h2>
    <div class="schematic-wrap">
      <div class="schematic-title">The tenebrism light model — Caravaggio's dramatic directional illumination</div>
      <svg class="painting-media-2" viewBox="0 0 520 170" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <rect width="520" height="170" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)"/>
        <defs>
          <radialGradient id="lg2" cx="14%" cy="18%" r="76%">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="#F5E8C0" stop-opacity=".9"/>
            <stop offset="42%" stop-color="#C8A050" stop-opacity=".35"/>
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#1A1410" stop-opacity="0"/>
          </radialGradient>
        </defs>
        <rect width="520" height="170" rx="6" fill="url(#lg2)"/>
        <ellipse cx="265" cy="92" rx="36" ry="44" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" opacity=".65"/>
        <ellipse cx="265" cy="58" rx="24" ry="28" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" opacity=".6"/>
        <ellipse cx="250" cy="56" rx="15" ry="20" fill="#F5E8C0" opacity=".28"/>
        <ellipse cx="279" cy="61" rx="11" ry="17" fill="#060302" opacity=".38"/>
        <circle cx="50" cy="26" r="9" fill="#FFF0A0" opacity=".85"/>
        <line x1="57" y1="31" x2="95" y2="53" stroke="#FFF0A0" stroke-width="1" opacity=".5"/>
        <line x1="59" y1="25" x2="98" y2="38" stroke="#FFF0A0" stroke-width="1" opacity=".5"/>
        <text x="65" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#FFF0A0" opacity=".85" font-family="sans-serif">light source</text>
        <text x="158" y="100" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" font-family="sans-serif">highlight</text>
        <text x="296" y="62" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" font-family="sans-serif">core shadow</text>
        <text x="392" y="120" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" opacity=".4" font-family="sans-serif">reflected light</text>
        <text x="42" y="148" font-size="9" fill="currentColor" opacity=".38" font-family="sans-serif">tenebrist dark</text>
      </svg>
      <div class="painting-text-9">Chiaroscuro (Italian: light-dark) uses contrasting tones to model volume and create drama. Caravaggio took this to its extreme — tenebrism — in which figures emerge from near-total blackness lit by a single raking light, with no transitional middle tones. Rembrandt used chiaroscuro with far greater subtlety to suggest interiority: the shadows in his portraits are not empty but inhabited, thoughtful space. The technique is the same; the emotional register is entirely different. Technique is never its own justification.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cscale-wrap">
      <div class="cscale-title">Colour symbolism: white — positive to negative valence across traditions</div>
      <div class="painting-text-5">White carries opposite meanings in East Asian vs Western European traditions. A student who learns European iconography and applies it globally will consistently misread non-Western works.</div>
      <div class="scale-head"><span>Purity / divinity / joy &larr;</span><span class="scale-head-line"></span><span>Death / mourning</span></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">European Christian</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-8"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Ancient Rome</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-9"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Dutch vanitas still life</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-10"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Chinese tradition</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-11"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Japanese mourning</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-12"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-poles"><span>&#x2190; Purity, divinity, joy</span><span>Death, mourning, absence &#x2192;</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- PART D -->
  <div id="partD">
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-d-movements-in-depth-what-each-reacted-against-and-what-it-invented">Part D · movements in depth — what each reacted against and what it invented</h2>
    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">The logic of art movements</p>
      <p class="cval">Every movement is defined by what it rejects as much as what it proposes</p>
      <p class="cnote">Art movements do not emerge from a vacuum. Impressionism rejected the smooth finish of French Academy painting and its insistence on historical or mythological subjects. Cubism rejected single-point perspective and the five-century project of illusionistic space. Abstract Expressionism rejected the entire European pictorial tradition after World War II, treating it as compromised. Understanding a movement means knowing what provocation it was answering. The movement that shocks in 1905 becomes the orthodoxy of 1930, which the next generation rebels against in turn.</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="western-painting-movements-timeline-1400-to-2000">Western painting movements — timeline 1400 to 2000</h2>
    <div class="painting-panel-3">
      <div class="pc-axis" id="pcAxis"></div>
      <div id="pcChart"></div>
      <div class="chart-note">Bars show approximate dominant period of each movement. Many overlapped substantially; start and end dates are scholarly conventions, not hard boundaries.</div>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="movement-explorer-select-one-to-examine-in-depth">Movement explorer — select one to examine in depth</h2>
    <div class="painting-panel-3">
      <div class="mvt-btns" id="mvtBtns"></div>
      <div class="mvt-panel" id="mvtPanel">Select a movement above.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- PART E -->
  <div id="partE">
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-e-the-masterworks-paintings-every-educated-person-should-know">Part E · the masterworks — paintings every educated person should know</h2>
    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Why a canon exists and why it is contested</p>
      <p class="cval">The canon is real, useful, limited, and worth knowing</p>
      <p class="cnote">A canonical work is one that rewards repeated study, has influenced many subsequent works, and encodes significant historical or aesthetic turning points. The Western painting canon is also the product of institutional power — museum acquisitions, criticism, auction markets — which have historically favoured European male artists. Both things are true: the canon is a genuine guide to important works, and it is incomplete. Knowing the canon is the beginning of art literacy. Each work below teaches something you cannot get any other way.</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="ten-essential-paintings-select-one-to-examine">Ten essential paintings — select one to examine</h2>
    <div class="painting-panel-3">
      <div class="work-btns" id="workBtns"></div>
      <div class="work-panel" id="workPanel">Select a painting above.</div>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="actual-physical-size-the-scale-of-famous-paintings-surprises-almost-everyone">Actual physical size — the scale of famous paintings surprises almost everyone</h2>
    <div class="scale-wrap">
      <div class="painting-text-8">Relative height of selected famous paintings. A human figure (1.8m) shown for scale. Most people are surprised: the Mona Lisa is smaller than a laptop screen; Guernica fills an entire wall.</div>
