        <text x="334" y="197" font-size="8" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">3.18m</text>
        <rect x="374" y="0" width="48" height="174" rx="2" fill="var(--accent-bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width=".5"/>
        <text x="398" y="188" font-size="8" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Night Watch</text>
        <text x="398" y="197" font-size="8" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">3.79m</text>
        <rect x="442" y="34" width="42" height="140" rx="2" fill="rgba(192,90,160,.18)" stroke="#C05AA0" stroke-width=".5"/>
        <text x="463" y="188" font-size="8" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Rothko</text>
        <text x="463" y="197" font-size="8" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">2.9m</text>
        <rect x="506" y="29" width="52" height="145" rx="2" fill="rgba(139,58,156,.15)" stroke="#8B3A9C" stroke-width=".5"/>
        <text x="532" y="188" font-size="8" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">Sistine</text>
        <text x="532" y="197" font-size="8" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5" text-anchor="middle" font-family="sans-serif">~14m (1/10)</text>
      </svg>
      <div class="painting-text-7">Scale matters to meaning. Rothko's colour field paintings at 2.9m tall surround the viewer's entire visual field — this is intentional and fundamental to how they function. Seeing a Rothko in a book eliminates this completely. Guernica at 3.49m high is overwhelming in person; the scale of suffering is literal. Vermeer's small panels reward close, intimate looking. Neither large nor small is superior — each uses scale as part of the meaning itself.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <!-- PART F -->
  <div id="partF">
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-f-the-market-and-value-how-art-becomes-money">Part F · the market and value — how art becomes money</h2>
    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">The art market is unlike any other market</p>
      <p class="cval">Unique objects, asymmetric information, and no official price discovery</p>
      <p class="cnote">Unlike equities or commodities, the art market has no central exchange, no standardised reporting, and no obligation to disclose prices. Most art sales are private — conducted through galleries with no public record. The public auction market (Christie's, Sotheby's, Phillips) represents roughly 50% of global art sales by value and is the only partially transparent portion of the market. The global art market was valued at approximately $67.8 billion in 2022 (Art Basel and UBS Annual Art Market Report). A single painting — Salvator Mundi, attributed to Leonardo — sold at Christie's in November 2017 for $450.3 million, more than doubling the previous world record and raising serious questions about the relationship between art market and geopolitical capital.</p>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="top-auction-prices-for-paintings-record-sales">Top auction prices for paintings — record sales</h2>
    <div class="painting-panel-3">
      <div class="painting-text-10">Top auction records for paintings (millions USD, hammer price + buyer's premium)</div>
      <div id="auctionChart"></div>
      <div class="chart-note">Salvator Mundi ($450.3m) is included but its attribution to Leonardo is disputed by many scholars. All prices include the buyer's premium (typically 25% on top of hammer price). Inflation-adjusted rankings differ — earlier Picasso sales would rank higher in 2024 dollars.</div>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="what-drives-auction-price-the-key-factors">What drives auction price — the key factors</h2>
    <div class="painting-panel-3">
      <div class="painting-text-8">Approximate contribution to final auction price (art market economists' estimates — non-additive and highly context-dependent):</div>
      <div class="dist-bar">
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-6">Artist identity</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-7">Provenance</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-3">Period/quality</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-8">Exhibition hist.</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-9">Market timing</div>
        <div class="dist-seg dist-seg-style-10">Size</div>
      </div>
      <div class="dist-legend u-mt-sm">
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot bg-teal-500"></div>Artist identity — 30%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style"></div>Provenance — 20%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-3"></div>Period &amp; quality — 18%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-2"></div>Exhibition history — 15%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-4"></div>Market timing — 10%</div>
        <div class="dist-legend-item"><div class="dist-legend-dot dist-legend-dot-style-5"></div>Physical size — 7%</div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-note">Provenance (the documented history of ownership) establishes authenticity and adds cultural cachet. A Picasso once owned by Gertrude Stein carries a significant premium. Exhibition history at major institutions (MoMA, Tate, Pompidou) signals cultural validation and is priced in by sophisticated bidders. A work with no exhibition history and a gap-filled provenance is a risk both aesthetically and as an asset.</div>
    </div>
    <h2 class="sec" id="forgery-detection-tool-how-detectable-is-a-fake-with-modern-forensics">Forgery detection tool — how detectable is a fake with modern forensics?</h2>
    <div class="calc-widget">
      <div class="painting-text-6">Forgery risk calculator</div>
      <div class="painting-text-11">Select an artist and the forensic method applied to estimate how detectable a professional-level forgery would be today:</div>
