    <h2 class="sec" id="part-g-q-and-a">Part G · Q&A</h2>

    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">1. What is the difference between a painting and a drawing?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Conventionally: drawing uses dry media (pencil, charcoal, ink line) and painting uses wet media applied to a support. But the boundary is porous — ink washes are both drawing and painting; many "drawings" use colour. The distinction is institutional as much as technical: the same mark reads differently depending on whether it is labelled a drawing or a painting and where it is exhibited. Historically drawing was considered preparation for painting — a status distinction Modernism eroded.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">2. Why did Western painting take over a century to develop accurate perspective, when the mathematics is not complex?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Because perspective was not a solution to a technical problem but a shift in cultural premise. Byzantine and medieval painting was deliberately flat — figures were sized by spiritual importance, not spatial position. The Virgin was large because she was important, not because she was close. Perspective assumes the viewer's eye is the organising principle of visual reality, which is a distinctly Renaissance humanist assumption. The "discovery" of perspective around 1420 was the adoption of a new value system about what painting should do.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">3. Is there painting outside the Western tradition that is equally significant?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Absolutely. Chinese ink painting (shuimo hua) has a continuous tradition of over 1,000 years with its own sophisticated theory of brushwork, tone, and space — and a conception of what painting is that has nothing to do with illusionistic depth. Japanese yamato-e, Mughal miniature painting, Persian manuscript illumination, and Aboriginal Australian dot painting are all fully developed traditions with their own canons. Western art education has historically marginalised these, a limitation being actively corrected since the 1990s.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">4. Why did oil paint replace egg tempera as the dominant medium around 1430?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Oil paint stays workable for hours or days rather than minutes, allowing painters to blend colours smoothly, create atmospheric gradations, and correct mistakes. Egg tempera dries almost on contact and requires laborious cross-hatching to build tone. Jan van Eyck perfected oil technique around 1430; within 50 years Italian painters had adopted it. The transition was cultural as well as technical: oil's capacity for illusion — convincing skin, soft shadows, reflective surfaces — enabled a new naturalism that the Renaissance humanist project demanded.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">5. Can acrylic paint produce results indistinguishable from oil?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">For dry finished surfaces: sometimes. For process and handling: no. Acrylic dries to a plastic film in minutes to hours and cannot be reworked once dry. Oil dries to a polymerised film over months and can be worked wet. Skilled acrylic painters (Gerhard Richter used both) can achieve optically similar results, but the mark-making process is fundamentally different. The surfaces also age differently: old oil paintings develop crackle from differential shrinkage over centuries; acrylics are too new to know how they will look in 400 years — a genuinely open conservation question.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">6. Is the "male gaze" concept in painting criticism legitimate or just politics imposed on art?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">It is a legitimate analytical tool, independent of political advocacy. Laura Mulvey coined it for film theory in 1975, but the underlying observation applies to centuries of Western painting: the implied viewer of a nude is almost always constructed as heterosexual male, and the depicted woman is positioned as an object of looking rather than a subject who looks back. This is visible in structural choices throughout the tradition — how bodies are posed, whether they make eye contact with the viewer. Acknowledging this is accurate art historical description. What one does with that description involves values; the description itself is empirical.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">7. If I don't know the iconographic programme of a Renaissance painting, am I missing the point?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Partly yes, but not entirely. You can genuinely respond to formal qualities without knowing that the lily means virginity or the dog symbolises fidelity. However, for much Renaissance and Baroque religious painting, the iconographic programme is so central that viewing without it is like reading a novel whose references you don't understand: enjoyable but incomplete. Both responses are real, but iconographically informed viewing is fuller. Learning iconography is perceptual expansion, not pedantry. The same knowledge that helps you read a Botticelli helps you understand why Duchamp's urinal was such a radical act: you need to know the rules to understand what breaking them means.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">8. Is the distinction between Impressionism and Post-Impressionism meaningful, or just a convenient label?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Meaningful but invented retrospectively. The critic Roger Fry coined "Post-Impressionism" in 1910 for an exhibition of works already made — to group artists who had moved beyond Impressionism's concern with light effects toward more structural, psychological, or symbolic ends. Cézanne, Gauguin, and Van Gogh share almost nothing in style but are all "Post-Impressionist." The term is useful as a chronological bracket but unreliable as a stylistic category — a common situation throughout art history, where labels are administrative conveniences that sometimes illuminate and sometimes obscure actual relationships between artists.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">9. Did Picasso steal African art when developing Cubism?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">The facts: Picasso encountered African masks at the Trocadero museum in Paris in 1907, and Les Demoiselles d'Avignon (1907) shows their formal influence directly. He never publicly acknowledged this debt during his lifetime. African art was displayed as ethnographic artefact rather than aesthetic achievement — a framing that benefited European artists by making it available as formal inspiration while denying its makers the status of artists. The word "steal" conflates aesthetic influence (universal and not inherently problematic) with the structural fact that African objects were frequently acquired through colonial coercion and displayed in ways that stripped them of cultural context. Both the influence and the inequality of that relationship are real and worth naming separately.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">10. Why is the Mona Lisa the world's most famous painting when it is not obviously the most accomplished work in the Louvre?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">The Mona Lisa's singular fame is substantially the product of its theft in 1911 by an Italian nationalist, Vincenzo Peruggia, who hid it under his coat and kept it for two years. Before the theft, it was a respected Leonardo but not uniquely famous. The theft made it world news for two years; when recovered, its celebrity was permanent. It was subsequently reproduced endlessly as a ready-made cultural signifier. The painting is genuinely extraordinary — the sfumato technique, the atmospheric distance, the compositional mastery — but its uniquely elevated status above all other paintings ever made is substantially an accident of crime and 20th-century mass media.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">11. Is Rothko's late work — rectangles of colour — actually painting, or a philosophical exercise using paint as a prop?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">It is both, and the distinction is not useful here. Rothko's late paintings are technically sophisticated: multiple thin, luminous layers of paint create a glow that cannot be photographed accurately. The rectangles are deliberately soft-edged, trembling zones that Rothko wanted to induce specific emotional states — he said he wanted viewers to feel awe before his paintings as before a religious experience. The "philosophical exercise" reading underestimates the material fact: these works only function in person, at full size, in the specific lighting conditions Rothko specified in studio contracts. The philosophy requires the paint. Removing either the idea or the material reduces the work to something it is not.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">12. Is buying art as an investment a good idea?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">The data is consistently unflattering. Studies (Mei and Moses, 2002; Korteweg et al., 2015) find that art returns, after transaction costs of typically 25-30% of hammer price in fees, underperform a diversified equity portfolio over long periods. Survivorship bias massively distorts the picture: only works that resell are tracked, and the most valuable are those where the artist became famous after purchase — by definition unpredictable. Works that never resell — the majority — effectively have a return of zero. Art can be a reasonable store of value for the very wealthy managing large capital, but the idea that it is an accessible asset class that consistently beats equities is largely a myth promoted by market participants who profit from transactions.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">13. How did Han van Meegeren fool experts into accepting his Vermeer forgeries in the 1930s?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">Van Meegeren solved the hardest forensic problem of his day: aging the paint. He discovered that adding phenol formaldehyde resin to his oil paint mix and baking the finished work at 105 degrees C produced a hard, old-looking surface that withstood the needle-tap test experts used to check for tackiness in recently dried paint. He painted on authentic 17th-century canvases, using genuine period pigments. He also exploited cognitive bias: experts expected undiscovered Vermeers to exist, and his "discovery" narrative confirmed what the field wanted to believe. He was detected only because his arrest for selling Dutch cultural property to the Nazis forced him to prove his innocence by painting another "Vermeer" in his prison cell.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="qa">
      <div class="qa-q">14. Why do galleries not display prices? Is it purely snobbery?</div>
      <button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button>
      <div class="qa-ans">It is partly strategic and partly structural. Strategic: galleries worry that prominently displayed prices create a hierarchy of attention — you stop looking at the inexpensive work once you know it is cheap. Structural: price in the art market is highly negotiable, and galleries prefer to assess buyers before discussing price, to ensure the work goes to a "good home" and to maintain resale value by controlling who owns it. This gatekeeping functions as class signalling. However, the practical effect is also that serious buyers expect a private conversation — price display would remove the dealer's ability to qualify buyers. The opacity is both commercial strategy and social ritual simultaneously.</div>
    </div>
  </div>





</ModuleLayout>

