    {l:'Fresco — minutes to hours', d:'Buon fresco must be completed within 4-8 hours while the plaster is wet. Michelangelo divided the Sistine ceiling into approximately 400 sections (giornate), each painted in a single working day. This hard constraint produces the monumental, decisive quality of fresco — there is no tentative mark, no revision, no second-guessing.'},
    {l:'Egg Tempera — dries in minutes', d:'Egg tempera is touch-dry within minutes. Painters build tone through hundreds of fine hatching strokes laid over each other across multiple sessions. Botticelli\'s surfaces, examined close-up, are a lattice of individual tiny marks — the blended appearance is an optical illusion created at viewing distance.'},
    {l:'Acrylic — 20 minutes to a few hours', d:'Acrylic dries in 20-40 minutes (thin applications) to a few hours (thick). Retarder medium extends this, but working time remains far shorter than oil. The fast drying allows rapid layering — you can apply 10 layers in a day — which is why acrylic is popular for large-scale works.'},
    {l:'Oil — touch-dry in 1-14 days', d:'Oil is touch-dry in 1-7 days depending on pigment. Ivory black and alizarin crimson take 3-4 weeks. This extended open time enables the wet-into-wet blending of Leonardo\'s sfumato and Vermeer\'s glazed luminosity. You can revisit a passage a day later and work into the still-soft paint. The medium gives the painter something oil uniquely provides: time to think.'},
    {l:'Oil fully cured — 6 months to 2 years', d:'An oil painting is not truly stable until the oils have fully polymerised — 6 months to 2 years for thick passages. This is why paintings should not be varnished for at least a year, and why conservation environments control temperature and humidity with precision. The slow completion of drying is part of why oil paintings\' surfaces have such complexity over centuries.'}
