  var formalData = {
    line:{
      title:'Line — direction, contour, and gesture',
      body:'Line in painting is not just outline. Diagonal lines create tension; horizontals suggest stability; verticals suggest aspiration. Ingres used precise, continuous contour line to embody classical beauty — the line itself is the aesthetic ideal made visible. Cezanne used broken, repeated, uncertain lines that acknowledge the act of looking rather than resolving it into a confident boundary. In abstract painting, a line is not describing anything external — it is the entire event.',
      example:'Compare: Ingres, La Grande Odalisque (1814) — line as ideal form, precise and final. Cezanne, Mont Sainte-Victoire series — line as visual searching, repeated and self-questioning.'
    },
    colour:{
      title:'Colour — hue, value, saturation, and temperature',
      body:'Colour operates on at least three independent axes: hue (what colour), value (how light or dark), and saturation (how intense or grey). Matisse was a master of colour as pure sensation: his Fauve paintings of 1905-1906 used non-naturalistic colours at maximum saturation to create emotional intensity without representational accuracy. Rembrandt reduced saturation to near-zero, working in ochres, umbers, and whites — yet the range of psychological expression is vast. Colour is relational: there is no fixed colour experience, only relationships between colours.',
      example:'Matisse, Woman with a Hat (1905) — a face painted green and orange as pure sensation. Rembrandt, Self-Portrait (1659) — almost monochromatic, yet extraordinarily expressive.'
    },
    tone:{
      title:'Tone — the architecture of light and dark',
      body:'Tone (value) is the single most powerful formal element for creating the illusion of three-dimensional form on a flat surface. A painting in pure unmodulated colour cannot describe a sphere convincingly; tonal variation turns flat shapes into volume. Tonal contrast creates focus: the eye automatically moves to the lightest point against the darkest background. This is why Caravaggio places the brightest light at the head — the viewer is directed without any explicit instruction.',
      example:'Caravaggio, The Calling of Saint Matthew (1599) — a shaft of light is simultaneously the compositional event and the theological meaning. Rothko, Seagram Murals — tonal values so close that the rectangles pulse ambiguously.'
    },
    texture:{
      title:'Texture — the physical surface as meaning',
      body:'Texture operates on two levels: depicted texture (convincingly painted velvet or glass) and actual texture (the physical impasto of paint itself, casting real shadows). Rembrandt\'s thick impasto in facial highlights creates a three-dimensional relief that catches real light as you move past the painting — completely invisible in reproduction. Frank Auerbach\'s portrait paintings have paint layers up to 2cm thick, built over years of overpainting, creating a topography as much sculpture as painting.',
      example:'Rembrandt, Self-Portrait with Two Circles (c.1665) — impasto of his cap casts actual physical shadows in raking gallery light. Ingres, Grande Odalisque — surface so smooth it denies its own making.'
    },
    space:{
      title:'Space — depth, flatness, and the picture plane',
      body:'Western painting from 1420 to 1900 largely concerned itself with the convincing illusion of receding depth behind the picture plane. Cezanne\'s innovations — simultaneous viewpoints, parallel modelled planes, no single vanishing point — broke with single-point perspective and opened the path to Cubism. Cubism shattered the window entirely, presenting multiple simultaneous views of the same object. Mondrian went further, treating the picture plane as a pure field of flat relationships with no depth at all.',
      example:'Vermeer, The Music Lesson (c.1662) — intricate perspective recession as optical achievement. Mondrian, Broadway Boogie Woogie (1942) — pure two-dimensional relational space, no recession whatsoever.'
    },
    composition:{
      title:'Composition — the arrangement that creates meaning',
      body:'Composition is how all elements are arranged within the picture frame — which element dominates, how the eye is led, what is placed at the golden-ratio positions, what is cropped. Triangular compositions create stability and hierarchy. Diagonal compositions create dynamism and instability. Degas placed his figures at unexpected angles, mimicking the casual snapshot aesthetic of early photography — entirely deliberate. The all-over composition (Pollock) eliminates hierarchy entirely: no edge, no centre, no stopping point.',
      example:'Raphael, The School of Athens (1509-11) — a perfect pyramid of 50+ figures organised into legible spatial hierarchy. Pollock, Number 31 (1950) — total compositional equality across a 5m surface.'
    }
  };

