  var mvts = {
    renaissance:{name:'Renaissance',dates:'c.1420\u20131600 \u00b7 Italy, then Northern Europe',
      reacting:'The gold-ground flat conventions of medieval and Byzantine painting \u2014 figures scaled by spiritual importance, no consistent space, no anatomical naturalism.',
      invented:'Linear perspective (Brunelleschi, c.1420); sfumato atmospheric modelling (Leonardo); high-key naturalistic anatomy (Michelangelo); oil technique in Northern Europe (Van Eyck, c.1430).',
      figures:'Leonardo da Vinci, Raphael, Michelangelo, Botticelli; in the North: Van Eyck, D\u00fcrer, Holbein.',
      example:'Leonardo, Virgin of the Rocks (1483\u201386): sfumato creates unprecedented softness through countless thin glazes. Figures emerge from shadow rather than being drawn in outline.',
      myth:'Renaissance painters did not simply "copy nature." They constructed idealised images according to Neoplatonic mathematics. Leonardo\'s figures are more anatomically perfect than any actual human body \u2014 philosophical ideals rendered in flesh, not observations of specific people.'
    },
    baroque:{name:'Baroque',dates:'c.1590\u20131720 \u00b7 Italy, Flanders, Spain, the Netherlands',
      reacting:'The restraint and clarity of High Renaissance painting; also responding to the Counter-Reformation\'s demand for emotionally persuasive religious art.',
      invented:'Tenebrism (extreme chiaroscuro \u2014 Caravaggio); diagonal dynamic compositions; visceral physical immediacy in religious scenes; the psychologically complex informal portrait (Rembrandt).',
      figures:'Caravaggio, Rembrandt, Rubens, Vel\u00e1zquez, Poussin, Vermeer, Frans Hals.',
      example:'Caravaggio, The Conversion of Saint Paul (1601): the miracle is shown through a horse\'s rump. Paul is knocked from his horse not by a visible divine vision but by an inexplicable internal event.',
      myth:'Baroque does not mean the same as Rococo. Baroque is dramatic, monumental, emotionally intense, and often religious. Rococo (c.1700) is light, decorative, erotic, and aristocratic. They share ornamental richness but almost nothing else in spirit.'
    },
    impressionism:{name:'Impressionism',dates:'c.1860\u20131890 \u00b7 Paris',
      reacting:'The French Academy and its Salon, which required historical or mythological subjects painted with invisible, smooth brushwork and academic finish.',
      invented:'Painting outdoors (en plein air) to capture transient light; visible expressive brushwork as a value; everyday modern life as the primary subject; broken colour placing complementary colours side by side to optically mix at a distance.',
      figures:'Monet, Renoir, Degas, Pissarro, Sisley; Manet was the influential precursor.',
      example:'Monet\'s series paintings \u2014 Haystacks (1890\u201391), Rouen Cathedral (1892\u201394), Water Lilies \u2014 show the same motif at different times of day to demonstrate that light, not the object, is the true subject.',
      myth:'Impressionism was not sloppiness. Contemporary critics attacked the visible brushwork as incompetent. The paintings require tremendous technical skill and a principled decision to show process. They only resolve into coherent images at the correct viewing distance \u2014 entirely intentional.'
    },
    cubism:{name:'Cubism',dates:'c.1907\u20131935 \u00b7 Paris',
      reacting:'The entire Western tradition of single-point perspective and the representation of objects from a fixed viewpoint \u2014 a tradition in place since Brunelleschi in 1420, abandoned in under a decade.',
      invented:'Multiple simultaneous viewpoints on the same object on a single canvas; the picture plane as interlocking flat planes; collage as a fine art medium (papier coll\u00e9, 1912); analysis of objects into geometric components.',
      figures:'Pablo Picasso, Georges Braque; later Juan Gris, Fernand L\u00e9ger.',
      example:'Picasso, Les Demoiselles d\'Avignon (1907): five figures shown from multiple simultaneous viewpoints, two faces replaced by African mask-derived angular forms. The first painting with no visual resolution.',
      myth:'Cubism was not about making things abstract or decorative. It was a philosophical claim: that visual experience is inherently temporal and multi-perspectival, and that a painting showing only one viewpoint is fundamentally dishonest to lived experience.'
    },
    expressionism:{name:'German Expressionism',dates:'c.1905\u20131933 \u00b7 Germany and Austria',
      reacting:'The decorative prettiness of Impressionism and the bourgeois optimism of late 19th-century painting; responding to rapid industrialisation and urban modernity.',
      invented:'Colour and distortion used to express psychological states rather than describe external reality; the city as alienating subject; the figure as a site of psychological crisis.',
      figures:'Ernst Ludwig Kirchner, Egon Schiele, Oskar Kokoschka, Emil Nolde, Max Beckmann; K\u00e4the Kollwitz is a crucial related figure.',
      example:'Kirchner\'s Berlin street scenes (1913\u201314): figures in harsh angular colours show modernity as threat. Schiele\'s self-portraits use extreme anatomical contortion to show interiority.',
      myth:'Edvard Munch\'s The Scream (1893) is often labelled Expressionist, but Munch was Norwegian and preceded the movement by a decade. He is more accurately a Symbolist precursor whose work directly influenced the German Expressionists.'
    },
    abex:{name:'Abstract Expressionism',dates:'c.1940\u20131965 \u00b7 New York',
      reacting:'European pictorial traditions entirely \u2014 treating them as compromised after the Holocaust. Also reacting against Social Realism of the 1930s and Surrealism\'s reliance on literary imagery.',
      invented:'Action painting (Pollock\'s drip technique); colour field (Rothko\'s immersive colour planes without shape); the large format as psychological environment; the artist\'s body as the scale reference.',
      figures:'Jackson Pollock, Mark Rothko, Willem de Kooning, Franz Kline, Lee Krasner, Helen Frankenthaler, Barnett Newman.',
      example:'Pollock, Number 31 (1950), MoMA: 2.7 \u00d7 5.3m of dripped paint. In reproduction it looks chaotic. In person, the all-over composition creates a total visual environment with no beginning, end, or hierarchical centre.',
      myth:'Abstract Expressionism was not random. Pollock\'s drip technique required extraordinary physical control. Rothko spent months adjusting the proportions and value relationships of his rectangles. Both were rigorous, deliberate artists who knew exactly what they were doing.'
