      ],
      look:'Find the small girl in the lower left. Then look at the captain\'s gesture: his hand forward into the light seems to emerge from the picture plane into your space. This illusionistic gesture was a technical tour de force in 1642 and a statement that no Dutch group portrait had made before.'
    },
    lasmeninas:{title:'Las Meninas',meta:'Diego Vel\u00e1zquez \u00b7 1656 \u00b7 Oil on canvas \u00b7 318 \u00d7 276 cm \u00b7 Museo del Prado, Madrid',
      boxes:[
        {l:'Physical reality',v:'318 \u00d7 276 cm \u2014 a room-filling canvas. In the Prado it hangs at a height that positions the figures at roughly life-size when viewed from the intended distance.'},
        {l:'Key technique',v:'Virtuoso loose brushwork that resolves into legible detail only at viewing distance \u2014 deliberately impressionistic marks create the impression of detail without actually describing it.'},
        {l:'Counterintuitive fact',v:'The King and Queen of Spain appear only as reflections in a small mirror on the far wall \u2014 subordinate to their own servants. This inversion of hierarchy is either courtly paradox or veiled commentary.'},
        {l:'Famous analysis',v:'Michel Foucault opened The Order of Things (1966) with a 12-page analysis arguing the painting is a meditation on representation itself \u2014 what it means to depict, to see, and to be seen.'}
      ],
      look:'The painting is fundamentally about the act of looking. Velazquez shows himself painting at a large canvas whose back faces us. Count how many directions of gaze are operating simultaneously. Then ask: who is the subject of the painting that Velazquez is making within the painting? The answer is unresolvable \u2014 which is the point.'
    },
    guernica:{title:'Guernica',meta:'Pablo Picasso \u00b7 1937 \u00b7 Oil on canvas \u00b7 349 \u00d7 776 cm \u00b7 Museo Reina Sof\u00eda, Madrid',
      boxes:[
        {l:'Physical reality',v:'349 \u00d7 776 cm \u2014 the canvas is so large it overwhelms the room. The monochromatic palette (black, white, grey) was deliberate \u2014 to evoke newspaper photography and refuse painterly beauty.'},
        {l:'Key technique',v:'Cubist multiple viewpoints applied to bodies in agony. No single compositional centre. The electric light bulb is deliberately ambiguous: both modern technology and an eye of indifferent observation.'},
        {l:'Historical context',v:'Painted in 35 days after the German Luftwaffe bombed the Basque town of Guernica on 26 April 1937. Commissioned by the Spanish Republican government for the Paris World\'s Fair.'},
